              "text": "57\n\nway Company to Manchoukuo from eleven cities and sixty-two towns and\nvillages in the S. M. R. Zone alone were estimated at 31,438,994 yen and\nschools transfered to the Japanese authorities, 20,708,000 yen.\n\nTogether with this gigantic transfer, a total of nearly 14,000 persons,\nincluding Japanese and Manchurians, had passed into the employment of\nManchoukuo, either directly or indirectly. Among them may be mentioned\n1,396 police officers and men from the Japanese Embassy, 3,723 police\nofficers and men from the Kwantung Bureau, 150 judicial officers, 1,500\npostal officers and employees of the Kwantung Bureau, 340 tax office officers\nand employees, and 6,884 officers and employees of the Local Administration\nBureau of the South Manchuria Railway Company. The last figure included\nan army of 2,663 teachers who were under the employment of the South\nManchuria Railway Company and who had passed into the employment of\nthe School Associations and Federation of School Associations.\n\nOf the above mass transfer of personnel, the largest number naturally\ncame from the South Manchuria Railway Company. On November 30, 1937,\ntherefore, solemn ceremonies were held at Dairen and all other centers where\nthe S. M. R. Local Administration Offices are located to bid an official farewell\nto the departing employees of the Company. And following this, the Local\nAdministration Bureau of the South Manchuria Railway Company, with its\nthirty-year history of brilliant pioneership and achievement, had been dissolved.\n\nThe negotiations over various institutions which the South Manchuria Rail-\nway Company has maintained for experimental purposes, which were to be re-\ntained by the Company for the time being, had progressed smoothly after the\nabove great transfer and a perfect understanding was reached between the South\nManchuria Railway Company and the Manchoukuo Government by March,\n1938. As a result, the second great transfer was made on April 1, 1938.\nIt involved the following institutions: (1) the Kungchuling Agricultural\nExperimental Station; (2) the Hsiungyaocheng Agricultural Experimental\nStation; (3) the Yamuyingtzu Agricultural Experimental Station; (4) the\nLiaoyang Cotton Experimental Station; (5) the Fenghuangcheng Tobacco\nExperimental Station; (6) the Taonan Agricultural Experimental Station;\n(7) the Linhsi Sheep Breeding Farm; (8) the Kungchuling Sheep Breeding\nFarm; (9) the Veterinary Institute; (10) the Geological Research Institute;\n(11) the Tumenling Forestry Experimental Station; and (12) the Hsingcheng\nHorticultural Experimental Station. With the above, the Health Research\nInstitute was transfered to the Kwantung Bureau and the Kwantung Army.\nThese institutions which served as the basis of various industrial activities\nin the past were evaluated at 3,637,000 yen but were to be \u201cloaned\u201d to\nthe authorities mentioned without compensation for the time being. With\nthem, an additional number of 445 S. M. R. employees passed into the employ-\nment of Manchoukuo or the Kwantung Bureau.\n\nThus passed into history Japan\u2019s extraterritorial rights in Manchoukuo\nand her administrative rights over the S. M. R. Zone. It is to the credit of\nall parties concerned, their spirit of cooperation and careful preparations,\nthat such an epochal transition of first magnitude was consummated with\nan amazing smoothness, even without a slightest hitch or difficulty. And\nthe satisfactory result of the procedure has strongly underwritten Japan\u2019s\ntrust placed upon Manchoukuo, as well as a phenomenal progress made in all\nphases of Manchoukuo\u2019s statehood during its short span of six years.",
              "type": "output_text",
              "logprobs": []
            }
          ]
